[2026-08-23 03:04:54] [cot:thought] The metric reveals severe US coverage gaps versus Korea. China and Europe show stable trends. US, UK, Brazil, Mexico, and Thailand are worsening as EV stock outpaces chargers.
[2026-08-23 03:04:54] [cot:thought] Ultra-fast growth in fast charging is widening these gaps. The country panel
[2026-08-23 03:04:54] [cot:thought] will span roughly twelve to fourteen markets. It will track BEV stock, public DC fast ports, BEVs per DCFC, and public charging capacity.
[2026-08-23 03:04:54] [cot:thought] IEA GEO 2025 and 2026 figures reveal stark divergences. China fast and ultra ports grew from 1.6 million to 2.2 million. US fast ports climbed from fifty thousand to seventy thousand. AFDC DCFC ports rose from about fifty thousand to sixty-eight thousand. European fast and ultra ports totaled roughly 148,000 in 2024.
[2026-08-23 03:04:54] [cot:thought] Korean fast and ultra
[2026-08-23 03:04:54] [cot:thought] ports grew from forty-seven thousand to fifty-one thousand between 2024 and 2025. Global electric light-duty vehicle stock reached roughly fifty-eight million in 2024 and seventy-six million in 2025. China accounts for about sixty percent of global electric LDV, or roughly thirty-five to forty-five million vehicles. Where IEA data does not separate battery electric vehicles from plug-in hybrids,
[2026-08-23 03:04:54] [cot:thought] the analysis will use electric LDV counts and flag BEV share estimates with confidence labels.
[2026-08-23 03:04:54] [cot:thought] The headline metric is BEVs per DCFC. The data module will be built comprehensively and committed in phase one. The ev-charging theme must be added to the registry and SLUG_THEME_IDS mapping.
